-- A stretch of time somebody spent in a book.
--
-- Rows are written while the book is open rather than when it is closed: a
-- reader who is interrupted mid-chapter has still read, and an app that only
-- recorded finished sittings would credit them with nothing. The row is opened
-- as soon as reading starts and its length is rewritten as the sitting goes on,
-- so what is lost to a process being killed is the last few seconds rather than
-- the whole evening.
--
-- `startedAt` is a wall clock in milliseconds, and is the one place in this
-- schema that trusts one. Everything else here counts writes instead - see
-- `ReadingPosition.sq` - because all it ever has to answer is which of two
-- things came later. This has to answer "which day was that", and no counter
-- knows what a day is. A device whose clock is wrong will file a sitting under
-- the wrong date; that is the cost of being able to say "you have read on five
-- days in a row" at all.
--
-- The one table here with no foreign key on `document`, and the only one that
-- should not have one. It outlives the book on purpose: removing a book from
-- the library forgets where the reader was in it, but the hours they spent
-- reading it happened, and a cascade would rewrite a history the reader can
-- remember better than the app can.
--
-- `deleteByLocation` is how a caller that means it takes those hours back.
-- Nothing calls it, which is also why `location` carries no index: an index is
-- a write per sitting, and this one would buy a scan nobody runs.
CREATE TABLE reading_session (
    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,
    location TEXT NOT NULL,
    startedAt INTEGER NOT NULL,
    durationMs INTEGER NOT NULL
);

-- Every reading of this table is "what did the last N days look like", which is
-- a scan from a date onwards.
CREATE INDEX reading_session_startedAt ON reading_session(startedAt);

-- Opens a sitting. Its length is zero until the first time it is extended,
-- which is what makes a book merely glanced at cost nothing.
start:
INSERT INTO reading_session(location, startedAt, durationMs)
VALUES (?, ?, 0);

lastId:
SELECT last_insert_rowid();

-- The length so far, rewritten rather than added to: the recorder knows how
-- long its own sitting has run, and adding deltas would drift by whatever was
-- written twice after a retry.
extend:
UPDATE reading_session SET durationMs = :durationMs WHERE id = :id;

-- The whole table, which is what the statistics are worked out from.
--
-- A row is three numbers and a path, and a reader who has read every day for
-- five years has a few thousand of them - less than one book's cover art. That
-- is cheaper to read whole than the several grouped queries that would answer
-- "today", "this week", "the last seven days" and "the longest run of days"
-- separately, and it keeps the arithmetic in one tested place rather than in
-- SQL that has to agree with itself.
selectAll:
SELECT * FROM reading_session ORDER BY startedAt;

deleteByLocation:
DELETE FROM reading_session WHERE location = ?;
